{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"07","chapter_title":"Data Storytelling With Excel","title":"Narrating a Data Finding","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting narrating a data finding to Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel","cat100_supplement_narrating_a_data_finding","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/03/06"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_calibration"]},"content":"# Narrating a Data Finding\n\n![Narrating a Data Finding textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/03-07-narrating-a-data-finding.png)\n\nA correct number sitting in a cell persuades no one. The analysis is only finished when you can say, in a sentence a busy reader will actually read, what you found and why it matters. Narrating a data finding is the step that turns a pivot result into a decision someone can act on, and it is where careful analysts most often slip, either by burying the finding in process or by overstating it for effect.\n\nThis section teaches a disciplined way to write the sentence. Lead with the finding, not the procedure. Attach the exact evidence (the numbers, the sample, the time window). Keep the verb faithful to the magnitude, so a 40% rise is not a \"surge.\" And label a correlation as a correlation rather than smuggling in a claim of cause. Each rule keeps your sentence honest enough that the underlying data would survive a skeptic checking it.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nYour analysis shows weekend sales averaging 700 per day against 500 on weekdays. You write it up two ways. The first: \"We pulled four weeks of sales and broke them out by day type to see whether there was a difference.\" The second: \"Weekend sales ran about 40% higher than weekdays (700 vs 500 per day, four weeks of March data), so shifting one staffer to weekends would match coverage to demand.\"\n\nThe first sentence describes your process and leaves the reader to find the point. The second states the finding, cites the evidence, and proposes an action. Same data, but only one of them is a narration a manager can use. Closing that gap, from \"here is what we did\" to \"here is what we found and what to do,\" is the whole task of this section.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA strong data narration is built from four parts, in this order:\n\n| Part | What it does | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Finding first | States the result up front | \"Weekend sales ran about 40% higher than weekdays\" |\n| Exact evidence | Anchors the claim in numbers and scope | \"700 vs 500 per day, four weeks of March data\" |\n| Faithful magnitude | Matches the verb to the size | \"40% higher\" (a clear lift), not \"skyrocketed\" |\n| Honest causation | Distinguishes correlation from cause | \"higher on weekends\" (a pattern), not \"weekends caused\" |\n\nThe most technical of these is the magnitude. A percentage change is computed as (new minus old) divided by old: (700 minus 500) divided by 500 equals 0.40, so 40% higher. Stating the percent correctly matters because readers anchor on it, and rounding 40% up to \"nearly double\" or down to \"a bit more\" both mislead.\n\nThe causation rule is the easiest to violate by accident. Your data shows weekend and high sales occurring together, which is correlation. Calling it \"weekends cause higher sales\" claims a mechanism the data did not test; a March promotion or payday timing could explain part of the gap. Naming the alternative explanation, and the limit it implies, is what makes the narration trustworthy rather than merely confident.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nSuppose your analysis produced this small summary table.\n\n```\n   A          B\n1  Day type   Avg daily sales\n2  Weekday    500\n3  Weekend    700\n```\n\nWeekend sales (700) are `(700 - 500) / 500 = 0.40`, so 40% higher than weekday sales.\n\nStep 1. The weak narration describes process: \"We pulled four weeks of sales and split them by day type.\" That tells the reader what you did, not what you found.\n\nStep 2. The strong narration leads with the finding and its implication: \"Weekend sales averaged 700 per day versus 500 on weekdays, about 40% higher (4 weeks, March data). Shifting one staff member to weekends would match coverage to demand.\"\n\nStep 3. Audit the wording. Do not write \"weekend sales skyrocketed\"; 40% is a clear lift, not a record-breaking surge, so keep the verb honest. Do not write \"the weekend caused higher sales\"; the data shows a correlation over four weeks, and a March promotion could also explain part of it. Name that limit.\n\nThe auditable narration: finding first (40% higher), exact evidence (700 vs 500, March, 4 weeks), faithful magnitude (no inflation), and a correlation flagged as correlation rather than proven cause.\n\nBuild the weekend-sales sentence one decision at a time. Predict each choice before the reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Write the weekend-sales narration without overreaching\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Weekend sales average 700 per day, weekdays 500. What is the percentage change, and how is it computed?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"40% higher: (700 minus 500) divided by 500\",\"20% higher: (700 minus 500) divided by 1000\",\"140% higher: 700 divided by 500\",\"Nearly double, so call it 90% higher\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Percentage change is (new minus old) divided by old: (700 minus 500) divided by 500 = 0.40, so 40% higher. Anchor the reader on the correct figure, not a rounded-up impression.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Which opening clause is finding-first rather than process-first?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"We pulled four weeks of sales and split them by day type\",\"Weekend sales ran about 40% higher than weekdays\",\"Our method was to compare day types over March\",\"After cleaning the data we built a pivot by day type\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Lead with the result. The reader should meet the finding (40% higher) before any description of how you got there.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The gap is 40%. Which verb keeps the magnitude faithful?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Weekend sales skyrocketed\",\"Weekend sales ran about 40% higher\",\"Weekends drove a sales explosion\",\"Sales went through the roof on weekends\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"40% is a clear lift, not a record-breaking surge. Keep the verb proportionate: higher or rose, never skyrocketed.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The finished sentence: \\\"Weekend sales averaged 700 per day versus 500 on weekdays, about 40% higher (4 weeks, March data); a March promotion could explain part of the gap.\\\" Finding first, exact numbers, honest verb, correlation flagged as correlation.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nNow order the four parts of a trustworthy data narration as a reviewer would assemble them.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Lead with the finding (the result, stated up front)\",\"Attach the exact evidence (numbers, sample size, time window)\",\"Match the verb to the magnitude so it neither inflates nor deflates\",\"Flag correlation as correlation and name a competing explanation\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nLead with the finding, back it with exact numbers, keep the verb honest, and flag correlation as correlation. If your first sentence describes what you did instead of what you found, rewrite it.\n\nAudit any data sentence against these three tests:\n\n1. **Finding-first test:** Does the opening clause state the result, or does it narrate the procedure?\n2. **Evidence test:** Are the supporting numbers, the sample size, and the time window all present and correct?\n3. **Restraint test:** Does the verb match the magnitude, and is any cause-language actually supported, or should it say \"is associated with\"?\n\nIf a reader could check your sentence against the data and find it inflated, the restraint test failed.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nA frequent misreading is mistaking process for finding. \"We analyzed four weeks of sales by day type\" feels like a conclusion but only reports activity; the reader still has to extract the point. The finding is the comparison itself (weekends about 40% higher), stated before any description of method.\n\nA second misreading is treating vivid verbs as harmless polish. \"Sales skyrocketed\" for a 40% rise, or \"weekends drive revenue\" for a four-week correlation, each smuggles a claim past the data. The honest verb for 40% is \"higher\" or \"rose,\" and the honest framing for co-occurrence is \"associated with,\" reserving causal language for when you have actually isolated the cause.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A workshop write-up reads: Attending the study-skills workshop raised midterm scores by 6 points (82 vs 76, n=30). Attendance was voluntary. Which test does this sentence fail?\" explanation=\"The verb raised asserts cause, but attendance was self-selected, so motivated students may both attend and study more. The numbers and the finding-first wording are fine; the restraint test fails on unsupported causal language. The fix is associated with a 6-point gap, plus a note on self-selection.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The finding-first test, because it narrates the procedure first\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The evidence test, because the sample size is missing\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The restraint test, because raised claims a cause the voluntary design cannot support\",\"correct\":true}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the four parts to a different finding: a study-skills workshop where attendees averaged a 6-point higher midterm score than non-attendees (82 vs 76, across 30 students). A weak write-up says, \"We compared midterm scores between students who attended the workshop and those who did not.\" It reports the comparison existed, not its result.\n\nRewrite finding-first: \"Workshop attendees scored about 6 points higher on the midterm (82 vs 76, n equals 30).\" Now attach the restraint. Six points on a 100-point exam is a meaningful but modest gap, so do not call it a \"dramatic boost.\" And resist \"the workshop raised scores\"; attendance was self-selected, so motivated students may both attend workshops and study more. The faithful version adds the limit: \"attendees scored higher, though because attendance was voluntary, motivation may explain part of the gap.\"\n\nThe finished narration leads with the result, cites 82 vs 76 over 30 students, keeps the verb proportionate, and labels the self-selection limit, so a skeptic can check every word against the numbers.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader audits a data claim the way a fact-checker does, separating what the numbers show from what the sentence asserts. Practice these moves on any finding you read:\n\n1. Recompute the headline percentage from the raw numbers and confirm it is stated faithfully.\n2. Underline every verb and ask whether it overstates the magnitude.\n3. Flag any sentence that implies cause and ask whether the design could actually support it.\n\nWhen \"sales jumped 50%\" makes you immediately check the two numbers and then ask \"compared to what baseline, over what period?\", you read narration as a claim to be verified, not a story to be enjoyed.\n\nThese fact-checking reflexes tie the narration skill back to the module concepts of evidence-based revision and bounded AI support. Map how they connect.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_narrating_a_data_finding\", \"cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong narration artifact pairs the finished sentence with the evidence and the limit, so the claim is auditable. The trace should show the finding-first headline, the numbers behind it, and the one caveat you chose to keep. A model line reads: \"Headline: weekend sales about 40% higher (700 vs 500 per day, four weeks, March); I kept the verb at 'higher' rather than 'surged,' and I noted a March promotion as a competing explanation so no one reads it as proven cause.\"\n\nThe weak version is a punchy sentence with no numbers and an unearned causal verb. The strong version lets a reviewer recompute the percentage, confirm the sample, and see that the language stops exactly where the evidence does.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe same narration discipline transfers from a number to a trend you describe in words. Suppose your chart shows monthly active users rising every month but with the increases shrinking. A careless narration says \"growth is accelerating,\" which the slowing increases contradict. The faithful version leads with the real finding (\"users grew every month, but the monthly gain shrank from 600 to 150\"), cites the figures, and resists the upbeat verb. The data shifted from a two-group comparison to a trend over time, but the rules, finding first, exact numbers, honest verb, no overreach, are identical, which shows you can narrate any finding rather than only the one in the worked example.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/03-07-narrating-a-data-finding-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"03.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\nA concrete case for that trace: Copilot in Excel (part of Microsoft 365 Copilot, as of 2026) will auto-draft a narrative summary of a pivot finding and offer to drop it into an email. Audit that draft the way this section audits your own sentence: if it describes the 40% weekend lift as sales having \"surged,\" reject the verb and keep \"higher,\" while accepting a useful suggestion such as attaching the evidence figures to the email summary, and log both decisions. Tool names and drafting behavior change quickly, so the durable skill is the restraint test, not the tool.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title for CAT 100 03.07. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_narrating_a_data_finding\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_narrating_a_data_finding","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_narrating_a_data_finding","cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel"],"stem":"You found that weekend sales were 40% higher than weekday sales. Which opening sentence narrates the finding best for a manager?","options":["We collected sales data for several days.","Weekend sales ran about 40% higher than weekday sales, so staffing should shift toward weekends.","There is a spreadsheet attached with the numbers.","Sales happen on many different days of the week."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A strong data narration leads with the finding and its implication. It states the comparison (40% higher) and what the reader should do with it, rather than describing the process.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_process_not_finding","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_narrating_a_data_finding","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_narrating_a_data_finding"],"stem":"Your write-up says 'Sales went up after we changed the homepage, so the homepage change caused the increase.' What is the reasoning flaw?","options":["There is no flaw; the timing proves it.","It claims causation from timing alone, ignoring that a holiday, ad, or season could explain the rise.","The sentence is too short.","It should use a pie chart."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Two things happening in sequence does not prove one caused the other. An honest narration notes the change is correlated and names what else could explain it.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_correlation_as_cause","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_narrating_a_data_finding","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_narrating_a_data_finding","ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"An AI tool rewrites your finding as 'Sales skyrocketed to record-breaking heights.' Your data shows a 6% rise. What should you do?","options":["Keep it because it sounds exciting.","Reject the inflated wording and state the actual 6% rise, since the narration must match what the data supports.","Increase the numbers to match the wording.","Add more adjectives to make it stronger."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A 6% rise is not 'skyrocketing.' The narration must stay faithful to the magnitude in the data. Reject AI wording that overstates the finding.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_overstating_magnitude","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_narrating_a_data_finding","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_narrating_a_data_finding","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"After stating your finding, what should the next sentence in a strong data narration provide?","options":["An unrelated anecdote to keep the reader interested.","The specific evidence behind the finding, such as the numbers, the source, and the time range.","A longer restatement of the same finding.","A list of every formula you used."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Claim first, then evidence. After the headline finding, the reader needs the supporting numbers, the data source, and the period covered so they can trust the claim.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_3_7_narrating_a_data_finding_process_not_finding","difficulty":"medium"}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"7e253c5a32d0a5f489b81f68956190362455acfaab4887fb97226f4e95bd7f11","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}